  "hypothesis": {
    "id": "hypothesis_1",
    "title": "Reconstructed Agency: The King’s Wife as Sovereign Archetype",
    "question": "How can a body assembled from images and histories that have objectified Black women become a being with agency and the power to define itself—while keeping the violence, desire, and contradictions of those histories visible?",
    "sources": [
      {
        "source_id": "artic_102611",
        "role": "Archetype of relational agency",
        "transferred_property": "Hierarchical composition: towering wife as guardian, diminutive king as dependent, symbolic crown as power source",
        "discarded_property": "Colonial framing of the king’s role as absolute authority"
      }
    ],
    "relationship": "The composition repositions the Black woman as the central axis of power, not as a passive subject. Her agency emerges through her relationship to the surrounding fragments, which are not erased but integrated into her form as memory, trauma, and resilience.",
    "transformation": "Adapts the formal language of Olowe of Ise’s Veranda Post—exaggerated scale, interrelationships, and open space—into a modern, fragmented portrait that turns objectification into a source of identity.",
    "composition": "A composite figure with layered silhouettes: a central towering Black woman (the sovereign) in regal pose, flanked by smaller fragmented bodies (historical fragments of objectification), while a crown floats above her head like a halo. Behind her, a translucent, fragmented silhouette of a colonial-era iconography (e.g., a slave trader or a colonial photographer) melts into her form.",
    "render_instruction": "Digital painting: oil on canvas texture, muted earth tones with accents of crimson and gold. The central figure is rendered with exaggerated proportions and dynamic posture, her gaze directed forward with quiet authority. Fragmented bodies around her are semi-transparent, revealing internal textures of old photographs, newspaper clippings, and colonial-era illustrations. The crown above her head is made of woven strands of gold thread and broken chains, symbolizing both power and resistance. The background is a gradient of charcoal to deep indigo, evoking the weight of history.",
    "visible_test": "The viewer should feel the presence of the central figure as both sovereign and survivor, while recognizing the embedded fragments as evidence of historical violence and desire.",
    "uncertainty": "The emotional weight of the work may be ambiguous: is the figure triumphant or haunted? The viewer must negotiate between reverence and resistance, as the work does not resolve the contradictions but holds them in tension."
  }
}

{
